/** @mainpage %Qore proj Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section projintro proj Module Introduction

    The \c proj module provides bindings to the [PROJ coordinate
    transformation library](https://proj.org) for the
    <a href="../../lang/html/index.html">Qore programming language</a>.

    PROJ converts coordinates between coordinate reference systems —
    for example from geographic @c WGS84 (@c EPSG:4326, degrees
    lat/lon) to a projected CRS such as @c ETRS89/UTM zone 32N
    (@c EPSG:25832, meters), which is required for any meaningful
    planar distance or area computation.

    Natural companion to the [Qore geos module](https://github.com/qoretechnologies/module-geos):
    @c geos does planar geometry, @c proj gets the coordinates onto a plane
    in the first place.

    @section projapi proj Module API

    Main classes:
    - @ref Qore::PROJ::ProjTransformer "ProjTransformer" — a transformer
      between two CRSs; forward and inverse transforms for single points
      and batches.

    Standalone functions:
    - @ref Qore::PROJ::proj_version() "proj_version()" — PROJ version string
    - @ref Qore::PROJ::get_proj_info() "get_proj_info()" — structured version info
    - @ref Qore::PROJ::great_circle_distance() "great_circle_distance()" — geodesic distance on the WGS84 ellipsoid (metres)
    - @ref Qore::PROJ::forward_azimuth() "forward_azimuth()" — initial bearing from point 1 toward point 2
    - @ref Qore::PROJ::inverse_azimuth() "inverse_azimuth()" — back-bearing at point 2 toward point 1

    Companion user module (shipped in the same repo):
    - @ref ProjGeos "ProjGeos" — `transform_geometry()` and
      `transform_geometry_inverse()` project every vertex of a
      @c GEOSGeometry through a @ref Qore::PROJ::ProjTransformer
      "ProjTransformer". Implemented on top of
      @c GEOSGeometry::transformXY() so there is no C-level coupling
      between @c module-proj and @c module-geos — load the user module
      only when you need interop.

    @section projthread Thread safety

    A @ref Qore::PROJ::ProjTransformer "ProjTransformer" object is safe
    to share across threads. Internally, the module creates a separate
    @c PJ_CONTEXT per thread and a separate @c PJ* per (transformer,
    thread) pair. PROJ itself requires each @c PJ to be used only with
    the context it was created under.

    @section projusage Basic usage

    @code{.py}
%requires proj

Qore::PROJ::ProjTransformer t("EPSG:4326", "EPSG:25832");

# Single-point transform. Inputs in GIS axis order (lon, lat) by default.
list<float> xy = t.transform(11.60448861, 48.12702028);

# Bulk transform — one PROJ call for many points.
list<list<float>> xys = t.transformBatch(
    ((11.6, 48.1), (11.7, 48.2), (11.8, 48.3))
);

# Inverse transform (single + batch).
list<float> lonlat = t.transformInverse(xy[0], xy[1]);
list<list<float>> lonlats = t.transformInverseBatch(xys);

# Geodesic helpers — WGS84 ellipsoid, no ProjTransformer needed.
float d = great_circle_distance(11.5581, 48.1402, 13.3696, 52.5251);  # 504476.9
float a = forward_azimuth(11.5581, 48.1402, 13.3696, 52.5251);        # 14.12
    @endcode

